 There is other services out there, but we will focus on GitHub here.

23
00:07:07,850 --> 00:07:22,840
 Some recap before we start here.

24
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,830
 Quick recap from yesterday.

25
00:07:24,830 --> 00:07:26,980
 Agile methods and Scrum.

26
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:32,370
 Agile equals small steps, past feedback.

27
00:07:32,370 --> 00:07:34,460
 And we welcome change as well.

28
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:48,100
 The manifesto has four values, 12 principles, and the Scrum has a product owner, Scrum master, developer, and so on.

29
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:50,720
 And we have events as well.

30
00:07:50,720 --> 00:08:01,130
 So sprint planning, daily Scrum, review, retrospective, artifacts, product backlog, sprint backlog, and increment.

31
00:08:01,130 --> 00:08:12,300
 And we also have Kanban, which basically is a board, WIP limits, continuous flow, basically.

32
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:24,870
 There's questions I just gave you.

33
00:08:24,870 --> 00:08:31,060
 All right, so why version control?

34
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:41,860
 So if you look at the file names here on this slide, has anyone maybe done this?

35
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:53,580
 Yeah, probably have.

36
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:57,000
 I mean, most of us probably have.

37
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,860
 Is version control by hand?

38
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:02,210
 And it doesn't work that well.

39
00:09:02,210 --> 00:09:05,150
 Which file is the latest here?

40
00:09:05,150 --> 00:09:09,180
 And what's the difference between version 2 and final?

41
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:10,880
 And who changed that?

42
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:11,520
 We don't know.

43
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:16,640
 So version control is a system that keeps track of every change for you.

44
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:19,360
 It gives you four things.

45
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:22,720
 So the first one is the full history.

46
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:33,250
 So for every change, you can see what changed and when, who did it, and maybe a message explaining why.

47
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:37,090
 Second is you can go back.

48
00:09:37,090 --> 00:09:41,850
 So if you break something today, you can return to yesterday's version that worked.

49
00:09:41,850 --> 00:09:47,830
 And third, many people can work on the same code at the same time.

50
00:09:48,470 --> 00:09:52,320
 And the system helps you combine basically the changes.

51
00:09:52,320 --> 00:10:02,530
 And lastly, basically a copy on another computer like GitHub is also a backup.

52
00:10:02,530 --> 00:10:07,480
 So if your laptop dies, your code is safe.

53
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:13,980
 So in the software industry, every team uses version control and almost all of them uses Git.

54
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:21,410
 There is other version control systems called SVM, but that's pretty old.

55
00:10:21,410 --> 00:10:26,370
 I haven't used it for years, but works almost like Git.

56
00:10:26,370 --> 00:10:27,650
 I think Git is better.

57
00:10:27,650 --> 00:10:48,800
 Git and GitHub, people have to mix these up.

58
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:51,290
 So just clear here.

59
00:10:51,290 --> 00:10:54,050
 You probably know this as well, many of you at least.

60
00:10:54,050 --> 00:11:00,890
 But Git is a program that runs on your computer, saves the history of your project.

61
00:11:00,890 --> 00:11:04,620
 It works basically offline.

62
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:09,600
 You can commit on a train with no internet if you want.

63
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:18,910
 And it was made back in 2005 by Linus Torvalds, the same person who made Linux.

64
00:11:18,910 --> 00:11:25,250
 Because it needed a tool to manage the Linux code with thousands of developers.

65
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:28,480
 And GitHub is just a website.

66
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:30,800
 It stores Git repositories online.

67
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:35,270
 So a team can basically share them.

68
00:11:35,270 --> 00:11:40,030
 So on top of Git, GitHub adds tools for working together.

69
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:45,810
 So for example, you have pull requests, code review, issues, project boards and so on.

70
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:48,920
 GitHub nowadays is owned by Microsoft.

71
00:11:49,910 --> 00:11:57,380
 There are alternatives like GitLab and Bitbucket that work basically in a similar way.

72
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:08,260
 A simple way to remember it is, Git is like the camera that takes the pictures.

73
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:13,210
 GitHub is like the online photo album where you share them with others.

74
00:12:13,210 --> 00:12:23,780
 So here's the vocabulary I want you to remember.

75
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:26,900
 You will hear these words every day in a developer job.

76
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:28,510
 So it's worth learning them.

77
00:12:28,510 --> 00:12:36,010
 Repository or repo, project folder that Git keeps track of.

78
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:41,130
 A commit is a saved snapshot of the whole project at the moment.

79
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:46,780
 Sometimes, most of the time with a message describing the change.

80
00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:55,330
 So think of it as a save in a game.

81
00:12:55,330 --> 00:12:59,360
 A branch is a separate line of work.

82
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:05,520
 A remote is a copy of the repo somewhere else, usually on GitHub.

83
00:13:06,300 --> 00:13:09,730
 Clone means downloading a repo to your computer.

84
00:13:09,730 --> 00:13:15,490
 Push sends your commits to the remote.

85
00:13:15,490 --> 00:13:18,750
 Pull gets new commits from the remote.

86
00:13:19,720 --> 00:13:22,650
 Merge combines two branches.

87
00:13:22,650 --> 00:13:30,170
 And the pull request is a request to merge your branch into another one, usually main,

88
00:13:30,170 --> 00:13:34,300
 with a review from your team.

89
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:38,490
 We might look at these in details later.

90
00:13:38,490 --> 00:13:40,140
 Yes, someone is raising hands.

91
00:13:40,140 --> 00:13:40,780
 Who is it?

92
