 Novika.

93
00:13:41,950 --> 00:13:43,070
 Novisa.

94
00:13:43,070 --> 00:13:45,280
 I think I said that right.

95
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:50,870
 Yep, raising hand.

96
00:13:50,870 --> 00:13:51,510
 Novika.

97
00:13:51,510 --> 00:13:56,960
 I can't hear you if you're speaking.

98
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:09,310
 I think something is wrong with you.

99
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:11,950
 Maybe you can ask the question in the chat.

100
00:14:11,950 --> 00:14:20,000
 And then raise your hand again when you have done that.

101
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:25,990
 Branch, one more time.

102
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:27,650
 Okay, let's go through it again.

103
00:14:27,650 --> 00:14:29,760
 So what is a branch?

104
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:38,100
 We will actually go a little bit more deeper, a little bit later in this.

105
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:41,280
 So we'll come back to the branch.

106
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:41,760
 Okay?

107
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:44,290
 So don't worry if you don't understand it right now.

108
00:14:44,290 --> 00:14:48,150
 Just remember this.

109
00:14:48,150 --> 00:14:57,670
 Okay, where were we?

110
00:15:03,710 --> 00:15:05,500
 Three places for your code.

111
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:07,870
 This is the most important idea in Git.

112
00:15:07,870 --> 00:15:09,950
 So let's take this slowly.

113
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:13,730
 Your code can be in three places.

114
00:15:13,730 --> 00:15:18,350
 So the working directory is just your files.

115
00:15:18,350 --> 00:15:23,450
 The ones you edit in, for example, Visual Studio Code.

116
00:15:23,450 --> 00:15:27,230
 The staging area is like a waiting room.

117
00:15:27,230 --> 00:15:38,750
 You use git add to choose which changes should go into the next commit.

118
00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:46,390
 The repository is the history.

119
00:15:46,390 --> 00:15:49,210
 All the commits you have saved.

120
00:15:49,210 --> 00:15:58,590
 You use git commit to take everything in the staging area and save it as a new snapshot.

121
00:15:58,590 --> 00:16:02,490
 So why this extra step?

122
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:06,690
 Because you don't always want to save everything at once.

123
00:16:06,690 --> 00:16:11,610
 Imagine you fixed a bug and started on a new feature.

124
00:16:11,610 --> 00:16:20,130
 You can add only the bug fix, commit it with a message, for example,

125
00:16:20,770 --> 00:16:29,410
 fixed price, rounding, or whatever, and commit the feature later.

126
00:16:29,410 --> 00:16:32,450
 So one commit, one change, basically.

127
00:16:32,450 --> 00:16:41,910
 So let's take an example, an image that maybe helps here.

128
00:16:41,910 --> 00:16:43,900
 You're packing a moving box.

129
00:16:43,900 --> 00:16:47,520
 Git add is putting things in the box.

130
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:52,830
 And git commit is closing the box and writing a label on it.

131
00:16:52,830 --> 00:17:06,120
 Okay, I have a question to you guys.

132
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:12,890
 Let's just say I changed three files, but I only ran git add on one of them.

133
00:17:12,890 --> 00:17:15,530
 Then I did a git commit.

134
00:17:15,530 --> 00:17:21,120
 What is in the commit is my question.

135
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:23,680
 Okay, I can repeat it.

136
00:17:23,680 --> 00:17:31,630
 I've changed three files, but only ran git add on one of them, and then git commit.

137
00:17:31,630 --> 00:17:33,500
 What's in the commit?

138
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:36,540
 Yes, Kristoffer.

139
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:39,680
 Yeah, that would be only the one file, right?

140
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:41,340
 Yes, correct.

141
00:17:41,340 --> 00:17:43,720
 Only the one file I added.

142
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:49,970
 The other two changes are still in the working directory, but not committed yet.

143
00:17:49,970 --> 00:18:00,030
 Now, some commands.

144
00:18:00,030 --> 00:18:05,530
 Remember, you don't need to type along now.

145
00:18:05,530 --> 00:18:08,290
 Just look at what's in front of you.

146
00:18:08,290 --> 00:18:21,120
 So first, once per computer, you tell git who you are, your name and your email.

147
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:24,350
 So every commit is stamped with them.

148
00:18:24,350 --> 00:18:28,010
 So the team can see who made each change.

149
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:32,800
 Use the same email as your GitHub account here.

150
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,240
 So that's important.

151
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:40,990
 The third line here makes sure your main branch is called main.

152
00:18:42,970 --> 00:18:47,230
 Yes, someone interrupting me here.

153
00:18:47,230 --> 00:18:50,290
 Sounded like a child.

154
00:18:50,290 --> 00:18:53,230
 I hope we don't have any childs in our class.

155
00:18:54,350 --> 00:18:56,110
 Then this is going to be hard to learn.

156
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:06,030
 Yeah, please mute your mic.

157
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:22,190
 Okay, I think Aladin, can you check that?

158
00:19:22,190 --> 00:19:25,840
 Yeah, sure.

159
00:19:35,550 --> 00:19:52,450
 So the third line, this line here, this makes sure our main branch is called main.

160
00:19:52,450 --> 00:19:57,950
 Older versions of git call it master, but GitHub uses main.

161
00:19:57,950 --> 00:19:59,710
 So we will do the same.

162
00:20:01,210 --> 00:20:02,850
 That actually rhymes.

163
00:20:05,490 --> 00:20:09,060
 Because back in the days, it was called master.

164
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:12,680
 Do you know why they changed it to main?

165
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:29,030
 I remember back in the 90s, having a hard drive.

166
00:20:29,030 --> 00:20:33,180
 I mean, installing a hard drive wasn't that easy, to be honest.

167
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:36,670
 It was pretty complicated back in the days.

168
00:20:36,670 --> 00:20:41,020
 So if you had two hard drives, one hard drive was called master,

169
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,390
 and the other hard drive was called slave.

170
00:20:43,390 --> 00:20:45,730
 I don't know if you remember that, if you're as old as me.

171
00:20:45,730 --> 00:20:52,540
 And we kept those analogies in IT, master and slave, master and slave.

172
00:20:52,540 --> 00:20:57,740
 But someone decided that we shouldn't use those words anymore.

173
00:20:59,900 --> 00:21:10,210
 So GitHub changed there from master to main instead, which I think is good.

174
00:21:11,700 --> 00:21:15,580
 It took a while to adapt to it.

175
00:21:16,860 --> 00:21:21,180
 But now we are getting used to main.

176
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:28,610
 Now, there are two ways to get a repository.

177
00:21:28,610 --> 00:21:39,020
 Git init turns the folder you are in into a new empty git repository.

178
00:21:39,020 --> 00:21:48,030
 And git clone downloads an existing repository from GitHub with its whole history, of course.

179
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:53,690
 In a company, you almost always start with the clone.

180
00:21:53,690 --> 00:21:57,470
 Because the project already exists, of course.

181
00:21:57,470 --> 00:22:05,320
 So if I ask you a question here.

182
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:08,220
 You join a company here on Monday.

183
00:22:08,220 --> 00:22:08,670
 Okay.

184
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:10,710
 Their code is on GitHub.

185
00:22:10,710 --> 00:22:13,150
 Which command do you use first?

186
00:22:13,150 --> 00:22:29,090
 Is it git init or is it git clone?

187
00:22:29,090 --> 00:22:33,630
 Yes, it's clone.

188
00:22:33,630 --> 00:22:34,750
 It's not init.

189
00:22:34,750 --> 00:22:37,800
 Because the repository already exists.

190
00:22:37,800 --> 00:22:40,790
 Init is only for brand new projects.

191
00:22:40,790 --> 00:22:45,390
 Okay.

192
00:22:46,110 --> 00:22:48,810
 Everyday commands.

193
00:22:48,810 --> 00:22:54,120
 I mean, these six commands are about 90% of what you will use every day.

194
00:22:54,120 --> 00:22:56,920
 Git status tells you what has changed.

195
00:22:58,510 --> 00:23:01,530
 What staged and which branch you are on.

196
00:23:01,530 --> 00:23:06,380
 And when you are unsure about anything, run git status.

197
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:10,400
 It often even tells you what to do next.

198
00:23:10,400 --> 00:23:13,750
 Git add stages.

199
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:18,180
 I mean, git add stages changes.

200
00:23:18,180 --> 00:23:19,700
 So one file by name.

201
00:23:19,700 --> 00:23:21,460
 Or everything with a dot.

202
00:23:21,460 --> 00:23:25,500
 But be a bit careful with the dot.

203
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:27,120
 Because it adds everything.

204
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:30,490
 Including the files you might not want to add.

205
00:23:30,490 --> 00:23:38,640
 And then you have this git commit with -m.

206
00:23:38,640 --> 00:23:43,520
 And that saves a snapshot with a message.

207
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:45,780
 So the -m here means message.

208
00:23:45,780 --> 00:23:51,040
 Git log.

209
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:53,890
 One line shows the history.

210
00:23:53,890 --> 00:23:55,760
 One line per commit.

211
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:57,980
 So each commit has an ID.

212
00:23:57,980 --> 00:24:01,340
 A shortcode like gibberish.

213
00:24:01,340 --> 00:24:02,230
 I don't know.

214
00:24:02,230 --> 00:24:03,110
 It could be anything.

215
00:24:03,110 --> 00:24:06,110
 A3, 5, 3, 21.

216
00:24:06,110 --> 00:24:06,430
 Yeah.

217
00:24:06,430 --> 00:24:08,190
 It's just a long number.

218
00:24:08,190 --> 00:24:19,020
 And git diff shows exactly which lines have changed.

219
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:25,280
 I mean, before you add them.

220
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:29,870
 Lines with a minus.

221
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:33,560
 You're going to see like minus.

222
00:24:35,030 --> 00:24:37,430
 That's the lines that are removed.

223
00:24:37,430 --> 00:24:39,510
 And lines with plus.

224
00:24:39,510 --> 00:24:41,730
 That's the lines that actually are added.

225
00:24:41,730 --> 00:24:47,760
 So the everyday rhythm is basically edit, git status, git add, and git commit.

226
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:48,560
 Over and over.

227
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:49,630
 Many times a day.

228
00:24:49,630 --> 00:24:50,750
 Sometimes.

229
00:24:50,750 --> 00:24:55,240
 Okay.

230
00:24:55,240 --> 00:24:57,250
 Let's just say you're about to commit.

231
00:24:57,250 --> 00:25:01,090
 But you're not sure which files you have changed.

232
00:25:01,090 --> 00:25:03,520
 Which command do you run?

233
00:25:03,520 --> 00:25:05,310
 If you're not sure.

234
00:25:05,310 --> 00:25:17,640
 Say diff.

235
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:19,610
 Yeah.

236
00:25:19,610 --> 00:25:21,730
 I mean, the answer is on your screen.

237
00:25:21,730 --> 00:25:23,460
 It's git status.

238
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:24,020
 Exactly.

239
00:25:24,020 --> 00:25:31,470
 But with git diff is to see the exact lines.

240
00:25:31,470 --> 00:25:34,140
 So you both are correct here.

241
00:25:34,140 --> 00:25:39,450
 Okay.

242
00:25:39,450 --> 00:25:40,410
 Good commits.

243
00:25:40,410 --> 00:25:47,640
 Commit message is a note to your team and to yourself in six months, for example.

244
00:25:49,150 --> 00:25:57,350
 Fixed stuff tells you nothing.

245
00:25:59,710 --> 00:26:01,310
 I mean, this is a bad example.

246
00:26:01,310 --> 00:26:03,790
 So fixed stuff here tells me nothing.

247
00:26:03,790 --> 00:26:05,370
 What stuff did you fix?

248
00:26:05,370 --> 00:26:07,630
 But here is a better one.

249
00:26:07,630 --> 00:26:09,790
 Fixed wrong total when cart is empty.

250
00:26:09,790 --> 00:26:11,070
 That's a better message.

251
00:26:11,070 --> 00:26:24,000
 The common style is to write the message like order.

252
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:32,350
 For example, you have add, fix, remove, or not added or fixing.

253
00:26:32,350 --> 00:26:38,490
 The trick you can have with you is the message should complete the sentence.

254
00:26:38,490 --> 00:26:45,790
 So if applied, this commit will so on and so on.

255
00:26:45,790 --> 00:26:51,290
 If applied, this commit will add category filter to products list basically.

256
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:58,370
 And commit often, in small pieces.

257
00:26:58,370 --> 00:27:00,300
 One commit, one change.

258
00:27:00,300 --> 00:27:03,670
 A small commit is easy to understand.

259
00:27:04,470 --> 00:27:07,930
 Easy to review and easy to undo as well.

260
00:27:07,930 --> 00:27:17,010
 A commit with a week of work in it is impossible to review and impossible to undo without losing

261
00:27:17,010 --> 00:27:17,600
 everything.

262
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:20,350
 So do small commits all the time.

263
00:27:20,350 --> 00:27:29,820
 Files git should ignore.

264
00:27:29,820 --> 00:27:32,000
 Some files should never go into git.

265
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:38,490
 And you list them in a file called gitignore with a dot at the start here.

266
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:41,190
 That's a very important dot gitignore.

267
00:27:42,530 --> 00:27:45,790
 And this should be in the root of the project.

268
00:27:45,790 --> 00:27:51,080
 So for example, pycache is a python cache generated automatically.

269
00:27:52,210 --> 00:27:53,810
 We don't need to save it.

270
00:27:53,810 --> 00:27:58,330
 And for example, .vnv is a virtual environment.

271
00:27:58,330 --> 00:28:01,610
 It's big and everyone creates their own.

272
00:28:01,610 --> 00:28:05,790
 And .log means all log files.

273
00:28:05,790 --> 00:28:10,740
 If you add .env, this is maybe the important one.

274
00:28:11,350 --> 00:28:16,310
 Because an .env file often contains secrets, password, database connection strings,

275
00:28:16,310 --> 00:28:17,990
 maybe API keys sometimes.

276
00:28:19,110 --> 00:28:21,340
 Those must never go to GitHub.

277
00:28:21,340 --> 00:28:24,850
 And here's why it's serious.

278
00:28:24,850 --> 00:28:29,250
 Because if you push your password and then delete the file in the next commit,

279
00:28:29,250 --> 00:28:31,010
 the password is still in the history.

280
00:28:31,010 --> 00:28:34,010
 Anyone can go back and find it.

281
00:28:34,010 --> 00:28:40,220
 And there are actually bots that can scan GitHub for leaked keys all the time, within minutes.

282
00:28:40,860 --> 00:28:48,930
 If it happens, the only safe fix is to change the password immediately.

283
00:28:48,930 --> 00:28:54,910
 GitHub can create a gitignore for you when you create a repository.

284
00:28:54,910 --> 00:28:59,650
 And you can actually choose the python template there as well.

285
00:28:59,650 --> 00:29:11,050
 Your computer in GitHub.

286
00:29:11,050 --> 00:29:14,680
 Now, maybe let's connect your computer to GitHub.

287
00:29:15,230 --> 00:29:21,470
 The remote is the copy on GitHub.

288
00:29:21,470 --> 00:29:26,780
 It's usually, its usual nickname is origin.

289
00:29:26,780 --> 00:29:35,630
 So git remote -v shows you were the origin points.

290
00:29:35,630 --> 00:29:39,790
 Git push sends your commit to GitHub.

291
00:29:40,610 --> 00:29:49,600
 So the first time you push a new branch, you write git push -u origin and the branch name.

292
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:54,650
 The -u connects your local branch to the one on GitHub.

293
00:29:54,650 --> 00:29:59,330
 So after that, plain git push and git pull are enough.

294
00:29:59,330 --> 00:30:01,310
 You don't have to do that anymore.

295
00:30:01,310 --> 00:30:04,310
 So git pull does two things here.

296
00:30:04,310 --> 00:30:11,560
 It downloads new commits from GitHub and merges them into your branch.

297
00:30:11,560 --> 00:30:15,710
 Git fetch, for example, only downloads.

298
00:30:15,710 --> 00:30:20,100
 So you can look before merging.

299
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:21,950
 One very important thing here.

300
00:30:21,950 --> 00:30:24,100
 Commit is local.

301
00:30:24,100 --> 00:30:26,740
 So when you commit, nothing happens on GitHub.

302
00:30:27,330 --> 00:30:32,160
 Your teammates can't see it until you push.

303
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:41,430
 And many beginners commit, close the laptop and wonder why the team can't see their work.

304
00:30:41,430 --> 00:30:43,580
 Well, because you haven't pushed yet.

305
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:48,610
 Signing into GitHub.

306
00:30:48,610 --> 00:30:51,870
 I mean, this is maybe a practical slide here.

307
00:30:52,750 --> 00:30:56,170
 Because this is where many people get stuck the first time.

308
00:30:56,170 --> 00:31:00,150
 When you push to GitHub, git needs to know it's you.

309
00:31:00,150 --> 00:31:05,820
 But your normal GitHub password does not work in the terminal.

310
00:31:05,820 --> 00:31:07,410
 So GitHub turned that off.

311
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:13,760
 I think it was in like three, five, I don't know, four years ago.

312
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:16,340
 And that was actually for security reasons.

313
00:31:16,340 --> 00:31:21,050
 On Windows, git for Windows includes git credential manager.

314
00:31:21,050 --> 00:31:25,280
 So the first time you push, a browser window opens.

315
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:29,250
 You log into GitHub and basically it remembers you.

316
00:31:29,250 --> 00:31:30,000
 Pretty easy.

317
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,840
 Other options are personal access tokens.

318
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:35,960
 Which is a long random password you create on GitHub.

319
00:31:35,960 --> 00:31:37,850
 And use instead of a password.

320
00:31:37,850 --> 00:31:39,560
 Or maybe an SSH key.

321
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:43,230
 A key pair on your computer that GitHub trusts.

322
00:31:43,230 --> 00:31:46,500
 So many developers actually use SSH.

323
00:31:47,450 --> 00:31:50,560
 I don't know if it's...

324
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:52,450
 Let me just check something.

325
00:31:52,450 --> 00:31:57,380
 So I don't say anything wrong.

326
00:31:57,380 --> 00:32:11,630
 Oh yeah, they actually removed the SSH part.

327
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:16,150
 Yeah, okay, so that's old.

328
00:32:16,150 --> 00:32:19,080
 But back in the day you could do that.

329
00:32:19,080 --> 00:32:24,970
 But you might have other problems.

330
00:32:24,970 --> 00:32:29,350
 Otherwise, I mean, most of you guys don't have these problems.

331
00:32:30,150 --> 00:32:33,190
 Because most of you already have GitHub and Git connected.

332
00:32:33,190 --> 00:32:36,650
 So now we come back to this.

333
00:32:36,650 --> 00:32:37,980
 What is a branch?

334
00:32:37,980 --> 00:32:40,220
 The big topic of today.

335
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:40,930
 Branches.

336
00:32:40,930 --> 00:32:44,130
 A branch is a separate line of work.

337
00:32:44,130 --> 00:32:45,640
 So if you look at the picture here.

338
00:32:46,910 --> 00:32:50,490
 Main has commits A, B and C.

339
00:32:50,490 --> 00:32:55,200
 Then someone creates a feature branch from C.

340
00:32:55,860 --> 00:33:00,190
 And commits D and E on it.

341
00:33:01,850 --> 00:33:05,150
 Meanwhile, main stays untouched.

342
00:33:05,150 --> 00:33:11,050
 So when the feature is ready, it's merged into main.

343
00:33:11,050 --> 00:33:12,960
 And that's F in this.

344
00:33:12,960 --> 00:33:17,010
 Well, why?

345
00:33:17,690 --> 00:33:19,850
 Because main should always work.

346
00:33:20,330 --> 00:33:22,730
 Main is the version that is live.

347
00:33:22,730 --> 00:33:24,870
 Or maybe ready to go live.

348
00:33:24,870 --> 00:33:26,750
 So if you work directly on main.

349
00:33:26,750 --> 00:33:28,430
 And push half-finished code.

350
00:33:29,150 --> 00:33:32,020
 You're gonna break it for everyone.

351
00:33:32,020 --> 00:33:35,480
 On a branch on the other hand.

352
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:36,520
 You can experiment.

353
00:33:36,520 --> 00:33:37,880
 You can make mistakes.

354
00:33:37,880 --> 00:33:40,600
 And commit half-finished work if you want.

355
00:33:40,600 --> 00:33:42,690
 Nobody else is affected.

356
00:33:42,690 --> 00:33:46,430
 Only when it's finished and reviewed.

357
00:33:46,430 --> 00:33:50,170
 Does it go into main basically.

358
00:33:50,170 --> 00:33:53,580
 So a comparison here.

359
00:33:53,580 --> 00:33:55,980
 Main is the published book.

360
00:33:55,980 --> 00:33:58,890
 A branch is your draft.

361
00:33:58,890 --> 00:34:01,810
 You don't edit the printed book.

362
00:34:01,810 --> 00:34:03,630
 You write a draft.

363
00:34:03,630 --> 00:34:05,470
 Someone reads it.

364
00:34:05,470 --> 00:34:07,190
 And then it's printed.

365
00:34:07,190 --> 00:34:16,660
 Now just take a sip of coffee.

366
00:34:16,660 --> 00:34:20,510
 Perfect.

367
00:34:20,510 --> 00:34:21,320
 Okay.

368
00:34:21,320 --> 00:34:23,160
 So here are the branch commands.

369
00:34:23,160 --> 00:34:27,960
 Git branch lists your branches.

370
00:34:27,960 --> 00:34:38,910
 The star here is basically when it shows the star and your branch.

371
00:34:38,910 --> 00:34:41,530
 It means this is the branch you're using right now.

372
00:34:41,530 --> 00:34:47,080
 Git switch -c features/search here.

373
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:54,810
 So git switch creates a new branch and moves you to it basically.

374
00:34:54,810 --> 00:34:58,780
 And the -c means create.

375
00:34:58,780 --> 00:35:05,170
 So git switch without the -c just moves to an existing branch.

376
00:35:07,550 --> 00:35:15,260
 And git merge takes another branch and merges it into the one you're on.

377
00:35:15,260 --> 00:35:24,930
 So to merge the feature basically into main you first switch to main and then merge the feature.

378
00:35:24,930 --> 00:35:37,760
 And when a branch is merged you delete it with for example git branch -d if you want.

379
00:35:37,760 --> 00:35:41,440
 And old branches are just clutter here.

380
00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:49,020
 You will see a git checkout in many tutorials and the stack overflow and all of that stuff on the web.

381
00:35:49,020 --> 00:35:54,010
 That's the older command and it still works.

382
00:35:54,010 --> 00:35:58,060
 But git switch is the newer and easier to understand.

383
00:35:58,060 --> 00:36:00,410
 So I recommend using that.

384
00:36:00,410 --> 00:36:05,350
 Okay.

385
00:36:05,350 --> 00:36:07,190
 So I have a question.

386
00:36:07,190 --> 00:36:13,860
 Let's just say I want to merge this feature /search into main.

387
00:36:13,860 --> 00:36:18,980
 Which branch should I be on when I run git merge?

388
00:36:18,980 --> 00:36:31,590
 Correct answer is main.

389
00:36:31,590 --> 00:36:32,740
 Exactly.

390
00:36:32,740 --> 00:36:35,740
 You merge into the branch you are on.

391
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:37,660
 Okay.

392
00:36:37,660 --> 00:36:46,160
 Here's the feature branch workflow.

393
00:36:46,160 --> 00:36:49,110
 This is the workflow.

394
00:36:49,110 --> 00:36:52,350
 So it's how most companies work.

395
00:36:52,350 --> 00:37:02,740
 And it's not all companies but most of them does this.

396
00:37:03,260 --> 00:37:09,050
 So every new feature or bug fix follows basically these steps.

397
00:37:09,050 --> 00:37:14,530
 So the first one we start from the latest main.

398
00:37:14,530 --> 00:37:17,660
 We switch to main and pull.

399
00:37:17,660 --> 00:37:21,630
 So you start from what everyone else has.

400
00:37:21,630 --> 00:37:23,330
 Very important.

401
00:37:23,330 --> 00:37:30,490
 Two, we create a branch for your feature.

402
00:37:30,490 --> 00:37:36,990
 Three, work and commit in small steps.

403
00:37:36,990 --> 00:37:41,470
 Four, push the branch to GitHub.

404
00:37:41,470 --> 00:37:43,680
 Now the team can see it.

405
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:44,450
 That's good.

406
00:37:44,450 --> 00:37:49,720
 Five, on GitHub open a pull request.

407
00:37:49,720 --> 00:37:51,470
 You're basically saying I'm done.

408
00:37:51,470 --> 00:37:53,950
 Please look at my code and merge it into main.

409
00:37:53,950 --> 00:37:55,690
 Okay.

410
00:37:55,690 --> 00:38:00,060
 Six, a teammate maybe reviews it.

411
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:04,210
 They read the code, maybe they comment, maybe ask for changes.

412
00:38:04,850 --> 00:38:07,900
 You fix them with new commits on the same branch.

413
00:38:07,900 --> 00:38:15,200
 And the pull requests will update automatically here.

414
00:38:15,200 --> 00:38:21,470
 Seven, when it's approved it's merged into main.

415
00:38:21,470 --> 00:38:23,360
 Usually with a button on GitHub.

416
00:38:23,360 --> 00:38:26,540
 And then you have eight.

417
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:30,970
 Do you delete the branch, maybe switch to main and pull.

418
00:38:30,970 --> 00:38:33,660
 So you have the merged version.

419
00:38:33,660 --> 00:38:38,900
 And then you start again from step one with the next feature.

420
00:38:38,900 --> 00:38:41,300
 So it's like a loop.

421
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:43,220
 We're going up and down, up and down.

422
00:38:43,220 --> 00:38:45,980
 So that's the workflow we need to learn here.

423
00:38:45,980 --> 00:38:54,070
 So if I ask another question here.

424
00:38:54,070 --> 00:38:58,470
 So why do we pull main before creating a new branch?

425
00:38:58,470 --> 00:39:06,730
 I can repeat it.

426
00:39:06,730 --> 00:39:10,650
 Why do we pull main before creating a new branch?

427
00:39:11,420 --> 00:39:20,600
 Yeah.

428
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:24,000
 So we start from the latest, said Christoffer.

429
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:26,770
 And many of you guys as well.

430
00:39:26,770 --> 00:39:27,090
 Yes.

431
00:39:28,320 --> 00:39:31,070
 So the new branch starts from the latest code.

432
00:39:31,070 --> 00:39:38,140
 Otherwise you will build on old code and get more conflicts later.

433
00:39:38,140 --> 00:39:42,060
 Naming branches.

434
00:39:42,060 --> 00:39:45,820
 Well, branch names should tell the team what's happening.

435
00:39:45,820 --> 00:39:49,030
 Many teams use a prefix.

436
00:39:49,030 --> 00:39:55,120
 Like a feature slash bug fix slash.

437
00:39:55,120 --> 00:39:59,200
 And then a short description with dashes, maybe.

438
00:39:59,200 --> 00:40:10,370
 Some teams add the number of issue or user story like feature slash 12 order history.

439
00:40:10,370 --> 00:40:16,670
 Then you can always find the story the branch belongs to.

440
00:40:16,670 --> 00:40:21,240
 Two rules that matter more than the name.

441
00:40:21,240 --> 00:40:23,250
 One feature per branch.

442
00:40:23,250 --> 00:40:23,740
 One feature per branch.

443
00:40:23,740 --> 00:40:28,340
 Don't fix the log in and add search in the same branch.

444
00:40:28,340 --> 00:40:31,740
 And keep branches short lived.

445
00:40:31,740 --> 00:40:34,280
 Maybe days, not weeks or months.

446
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:36,450
 A couple of days should be enough.

447
00:40:36,450 --> 00:40:41,310
 A branch that lives for two months is far away from Maine.

448
00:40:42,010 --> 00:40:45,370
 And merging it will probably be pretty painful.

449
00:40:45,370 --> 00:40:54,270
 And your team will have its own naming rules and just follow them.

450
00:40:54,270 --> 00:40:59,580
 The important thing is that everyone does the same thing.

451
00:40:59,580 --> 00:41:10,080
 Whole request or a PR is the center of teamwork on GitHub.

452
00:41:10,080 --> 00:41:13,990
 It's a page on GitHub that says here is my branch.

453
00:41:13,990 --> 00:41:16,800
 Please review it and merge it into Maine.

454
00:41:16,800 --> 00:41:22,020
 A good PR has a clear title and short description.

455
00:41:23,010 --> 00:41:25,500
 What did you change and why?

456
00:41:25,500 --> 00:41:30,010
 If there is something special to test, write it here please.

457
00:41:30,010 --> 00:41:34,480
 GitHub shows every change line in green and red.

458
00:41:35,500 --> 00:41:38,650
 Reviewers can comment on a specific line.

459
00:41:38,650 --> 00:41:40,140
 Ask questions maybe.

460
00:41:40,140 --> 00:41:42,370
 Suggest maybe changes.

461
00:41:42,370 --> 00:41:44,320
 And finally approve it.

462
00:41:44,320 --> 00:41:51,040
 Many teams also have automatic checks that runs on every PR request.

463
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:52,720
 For example the tests.

464
00:41:54,290 --> 00:41:59,810
 If a test fails you see a red cross before anyone merges.

465
00:41:59,810 --> 00:42:03,230
 And the nice trick here.

466
00:42:03,230 --> 00:42:08,170
 If you write closes #12 in the description.

467
00:42:08,810 --> 00:42:13,170
 GitHub closes issue #12 automatically when the PR is merged.

468
00:42:13,170 --> 00:42:17,730
 That links basically your code to the user story.

469
00:42:17,730 --> 00:42:21,320
 The name is a bit confusing.

470
00:42:22,440 --> 00:42:26,210
 You are asking the team to pull your changes into Maine.

471
00:42:26,210 --> 00:42:31,410
 GitLab calls the same thing a merge request.

472
00:42:31,410 --> 00:42:35,030
 Which is maybe clearer than a pull request.

473
00:42:35,680 --> 00:42:41,760
 Okay so another question is what should you write in a pull request description.

474
00:42:41,760 --> 00:42:43,900
 Do you remember that?

475
00:42:43,900 --> 00:42:57,230
 Yeah.

476
00:42:57,230 --> 00:43:06,910
 What we should write in the description is basically what was changed.

477
00:43:06,910 --> 00:43:07,490
 Why.

478
00:43:07,490 --> 00:43:09,090
 How to test it.

479
00:43:09,090 --> 00:43:12,800
 And the link to the issue/story for example.

480
00:43:12,800 --> 00:43:22,490
 Review means another developer reads your code before it goes into Maine.

481
00:43:22,490 --> 00:43:26,220
 It's one of the best ways to actually find bugs.

482
00:43:26,220 --> 00:43:30,280
 The best way also to learn.

483
00:43:30,280 --> 00:43:34,420
 As a reviewer be kind and specific.

484
00:43:34,420 --> 00:43:37,520
 Not like don't write like this is bad.

485
00:43:39,070 --> 00:43:42,350
 But this loop runs once per product.

486
00:43:42,350 --> 00:43:45,390
 Could we do it with one query instead?

487
00:43:45,390 --> 00:43:48,190
 Ask questions instead of giving orders.

488
00:43:49,730 --> 00:43:53,980
 Like check that it works.

489
00:43:55,130 --> 00:43:56,520
 That it's readable.

490
00:43:56,520 --> 00:44:03,060
 And that meets the definition of done basically.

491
00:44:03,060 --> 00:44:06,220
 As the author keep the pull request small.

492
00:44:06,220 --> 00:44:13,180
 A pull request with 50 changed lines gets a careful review.

493
00:44:13,180 --> 00:44:19,880
 A PR with 2000 lines looks good to me.

494
00:44:19,880 --> 00:44:21,460
 Because nobody can read it all.

495
00:44:22,780 --> 00:44:25,640
 And don't take comments personally here.

496
00:44:26,420 --> 00:44:28,020
 The review is about the code.

497
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:28,740
 Not about you.

498
00:44:29,280 --> 00:44:33,020
 Even senior developers get many comments on their PRs.

499
00:44:33,020 --> 00:44:39,820
 So as for example as a junior you will you will learn a lot from reviews of your code.

500
00:44:40,620 --> 00:44:45,850
 And you're also allowed to review seniors code as well.

501
00:44:45,850 --> 00:44:49,240
 Asking for example questions like why does this work?

502
00:44:50,140 --> 00:44:53,410
 Is a value valuable review comment actually.

503
00:44:53,410 --> 00:45:00,170
 Merging a pull request.

504
00:45:00,170 --> 00:45:03,930
 When a pull request is approved someone clicks merge.

505
00:45:03,930 --> 00:45:08,130
 GitHub back has actually three options.

506
00:45:08,130 --> 00:45:11,110
 Create a merge commit.

507
00:45:12,250 --> 00:45:15,260
 All the commits from the branch are kept.

508
00:45:15,260 --> 00:45:17,370
 Plus an extra merge commit.

509
00:45:17,370 --> 00:45:20,080
 You see the full history.

510
00:45:20,080 --> 00:45:22,110
 But it can get messy.

511
00:45:22,110 --> 00:45:24,930
 Okay.

512
00:45:24,930 --> 00:45:28,490
 Quash and merge.

513
00:45:28,490 --> 00:45:35,420
 All the commits from the branch become one single commit in main.

514
00:45:35,420 --> 00:45:38,950
 That's popular because main gets a clean history.

515
00:45:38,950 --> 00:45:40,710
 So one commit per feature.

516
00:45:42,220 --> 00:45:48,990
 Your fix typo or and maybe and oops commits basically disappear.

517
00:45:49,800 --> 00:45:53,080
 Then you have rebase and merge.

518
00:45:54,380 --> 00:46:00,340
 The commits are moved on top of main one by one without a merge commit.

519
00:46:00,340 --> 00:46:03,990
 So it's a straight line of history.

520
00:46:03,990 --> 00:46:06,790
 So you don't need to master the differences right now.

521
00:46:06,790 --> 00:46:10,330
 Your team will have chosen one.

522
00:46:10,330 --> 00:46:14,920
 Just recognize the three buttons here basically.

523
00:46:14,920 --> 00:46:21,140
 But sometimes we can have merge conflicts.

524
00:46:21,140 --> 00:46:23,550
 And then sooner or later you will see this.

525
00:46:23,550 --> 00:46:26,650
 It looks scary.

526
00:46:26,650 --> 00:46:28,860
 But it's normal and not dangerous.

527
00:46:30,480 --> 00:46:35,230
 It happens when two branches changes the same line in different ways.

528
00:46:35,230 --> 00:46:40,550
 So here main says welcome to the shop.

529
00:46:40,550 --> 00:46:43,970
 And the feature branch says welcome to our web shop.

530
00:46:43,970 --> 00:46:47,970
 Can't know which one is right.

531
00:46:47,970 --> 00:46:51,420
 So instead of guessing it stops and it asks you.

532
00:46:51,420 --> 00:46:56,110
 And git marks the conflict in the file.

533
00:46:57,600 --> 00:47:04,190
 You see how between the arrows and the equal signs is in one version.

534
00:47:04,190 --> 00:47:06,110
 The one from main.

535
00:47:06,110 --> 00:47:10,790
 And between the equal signs and the other arrows is the other version.

536
00:47:10,790 --> 00:47:12,720
 From the feature branch.

537
00:47:12,720 --> 00:47:17,090
 So to fix this you open the file.

538
00:47:17,090 --> 00:47:19,690
 Decide what the code should be.

539
00:47:19,690 --> 00:47:22,880
 Maybe one version or maybe the other.

540
00:47:22,880 --> 00:47:25,210
 Maybe a mix of both.

541
00:47:25,210 --> 00:47:31,030
 Delete maybe the marker lines.

542
00:47:31,030 --> 00:47:33,810
 The arrows and the equal signs.

543
00:47:33,810 --> 00:47:34,640
 Save.

544
00:47:34,640 --> 00:47:38,400
 Then git add the file and git commit.

545
00:47:38,400 --> 00:47:41,060
 And then you're done.

546
00:47:41,060 --> 00:47:44,180
 Actually Visual Studio Code helps a lot.

547
00:47:44,900 --> 00:47:48,580
 It shows buttons above the conflict.

548
00:47:48,580 --> 00:47:51,940
 Accept current change or accept incoming change.

549
00:47:51,940 --> 00:47:53,300
 Or maybe accept both.

550
00:47:53,940 --> 00:47:59,060
 And GitHub can show and fix simple conflicts even in the browser as well.

551
00:48:00,090 --> 00:48:05,090
 An important thing here is a conflict is not an error.

552
00:48:05,090 --> 00:48:06,380
 Nothing is lost.

553
00:48:06,380 --> 00:48:09,580
 Git is just asking for a human decision here.

554
00:48:09,580 --> 00:48:16,630
 Okay let's just.

555
00:48:17,270 --> 00:48:18,310
 I have another question.

556
00:48:18,310 --> 00:48:24,240
 Let's just say you have two developers change to different files on their branches.

557
00:48:24,960 --> 00:48:29,210
 Will there be a conflict when both are merged?

558
00:48:29,210 --> 00:48:44,180
 Yeah Fabian you're really fast.

559
00:48:44,180 --> 00:48:46,880
 Fabian is right.

560
00:48:46,880 --> 00:48:47,820
 No.

561
00:48:47,820 --> 00:48:50,970
 I mean git merges different files.

562
00:48:50,970 --> 00:48:54,970
 And even different parts of the same file automatically.

563
00:48:54,970 --> 00:48:59,830
 Conflicts only happen when the same lines are changed.

564
00:48:59,830 --> 00:49:01,790
 Like in the example here.

565
00:49:01,790 --> 00:49:09,600
 You can't avoid conflicts completely.

566
00:49:09,600 --> 00:49:13,120
 But you can make them small and rare.

567
00:49:13,120 --> 00:49:16,450
 Pull main often.

568
00:49:16,450 --> 00:49:20,220
 If your branch lives for a few days.

569
00:49:20,970 --> 00:49:25,890
 Bring the latest main into it regularly.

570
00:49:25,890 --> 00:49:28,140
 I mean switch to main and pull.

571
00:49:28,940 --> 00:49:33,500
 Switch back to your branch and merge main into it.

572
00:49:33,500 --> 00:49:40,020
 Then you solve a small conflict early instead of one big one at the end.

573
00:49:42,590 --> 00:49:45,810
 And keep branches small and short.

574
00:49:45,810 --> 00:49:52,130
 The longer a branch lives, the further it drifts away from main.

575
00:49:52,130 --> 00:49:53,410
 Remember that.

576
00:49:53,410 --> 00:49:55,090
 And talk to each other.

577
00:49:55,090 --> 00:49:59,780
 I mean yesterday's daily scrum is perfect for this.

578
00:50:00,340 --> 00:50:02,660
 I'm working on the login page today.

579
00:50:03,340 --> 00:50:07,910
 Then nobody else starts changing the same file.

580
00:50:07,910 --> 00:50:09,500
 If I say that to the team.

581
00:50:09,500 --> 00:50:15,360
 And also don't reformat whole files.

582
00:50:15,360 --> 00:50:19,520
 If your editor changes the indentation in a file of 500 lines.

583
00:50:21,050 --> 00:50:26,880
 Every one of those lines is a possible conflict for someone else.

584
00:50:26,880 --> 00:50:30,490
 And commit and push often.

585
00:50:30,490 --> 00:50:32,000
 So your work is visible.

586
00:50:32,000 --> 00:50:41,950
 Many teams don't just hope that everyone follows the workflow.

587
00:50:41,950 --> 00:50:43,870
 They make GitHub enforce it.

588
00:50:44,520 --> 00:50:46,520
 With rules on the main branch.

589
00:50:46,520 --> 00:50:50,960
 The rules is nobody can push directly into main.

590
00:50:50,960 --> 00:50:52,320
 Not even the boss.

591
00:50:52,320 --> 00:50:56,510
 All changes must come through a pull request.

592
00:50:57,570 --> 00:51:03,580
 So a pull request needs at least one approval from someone else.

593
00:51:03,580 --> 00:51:09,130
 And all automatic checks like the tests and so on must pass.

594
00:51:09,130 --> 00:51:12,510
 Then the main is protected.

595
00:51:12,510 --> 00:51:14,690
 You can't break it by mistake.

596
00:51:14,690 --> 00:51:18,410
 Because every change has been reviewed and tested.

597
00:51:18,410 --> 00:51:26,780
 You can set this in the repository settings on GitHub.

598
00:51:26,780 --> 00:51:29,430
 Under branches or rules.

599
00:51:29,430 --> 00:51:33,520
 So I recommend trying that.

600
00:51:33,520 --> 00:51:37,740
 Okay.

601
00:51:37,740 --> 00:51:39,260
 Git and scrum together.

602
00:51:39,260 --> 00:51:41,960
 Let's connect today with yesterday.

603
00:51:41,960 --> 00:51:45,530
 A user story becomes an issue on GitHub.

604
00:51:45,530 --> 00:51:50,410
 The sprint backlog can be a board in GitHub projects.

605
00:51:51,230 --> 00:51:56,130
 With columns like for example to-do, in progress, review and done.

606
00:51:56,130 --> 00:51:59,230
 So when you start working on a story.

607
00:51:59,230 --> 00:52:01,160
 You create a feature branch.

608
00:52:01,160 --> 00:52:04,670
 Maybe with the issue number in the name as well.

609
00:52:04,670 --> 00:52:06,600
 And when you're done.

610
00:52:06,600 --> 00:52:08,600
 You open a pull request.

611
00:52:08,600 --> 00:52:13,630
 With closes and the issue number.

612
00:52:13,630 --> 00:52:19,360
 So the review is part of the definition of done basically.

613
00:52:19,360 --> 00:52:23,360
 And when it's merged, the issue closes.

614
00:52:24,060 --> 00:52:25,500
 The cards moves to done.

615
00:52:26,210 --> 00:52:32,450
 And the code is part of the increment you show at the sprint review for example.

616
00:52:32,450 --> 00:52:41,530
 So the board, the branches and the pull requests all the same story.

617
00:52:41,530 --> 00:52:46,430
 That's how a real team works from day to day basically.

618
00:52:46,430 --> 00:52:52,800
 Oops.

619
00:52:52,800 --> 00:52:53,660
 I'm doing things.

620
00:52:54,220 --> 00:52:56,420
 Everyone makes mistakes.

621
00:52:56,420 --> 00:53:00,700
 Git has many ways to undo them.

622
00:53:00,700 --> 00:53:03,420
 Here are at least the safe ones.

623
00:53:04,940 --> 00:53:14,620
 Git restore with a file name throws away the changes you have made in that file since the last commit.

624
00:53:14,620 --> 00:53:31,420
 So the second one here takes a file out of the staging area.

625
00:53:31,420 --> 00:53:36,820
 But it keeps your changes in that file.

626
00:53:36,820 --> 00:53:42,770
 Git commit -amend -m here fixes the last commit.

627
00:53:42,770 --> 00:53:44,770
 For example, a typo in the message.

628
00:53:45,800 --> 00:53:47,320
 Maybe you wrote something wrong.

629
00:53:47,320 --> 00:53:49,440
 Or a file you forgot to add.

630
00:53:49,440 --> 00:53:55,570
 But this will work only before you actually pushed it.

631
00:53:55,570 --> 00:53:57,310
 So don't do push.

632
00:53:57,310 --> 00:54:06,970
 Git revert, as you can see here, creates a new commit that does the opposite of an old one.

633
00:54:06,970 --> 00:54:12,380
 That's the safe way to undo something that's already on GitHub.

634
00:54:12,380 --> 00:54:14,560
 Because history isn't rewritten.

635
00:54:14,560 --> 00:54:20,660
 Everyone can see that a change was made and then undone.

636
00:54:20,660 --> 00:54:30,700
 Two commits to be very careful with is git reset -hard and git push -force.

637
00:54:31,580 --> 00:54:33,210
 They can actually delete work.

638
00:54:35,210 --> 00:54:37,630
 Yours, maybe your work or your teammates.

639
00:54:37,630 --> 00:54:44,730
 So don't use them on shared branches unless you really know what you're doing.

640
00:54:44,730 --> 00:54:55,970
 And remember, sum up this morning here.

641
00:54:55,970 --> 00:54:58,820
 Git saves the history of your code.

642
00:54:59,920 --> 00:55:03,370
 GitHub shares it with your team every day.

643
00:55:04,170 --> 00:55:08,190
 Git status, git add commit push and pull.

644
00:55:08,190 --> 00:55:13,460
 Commit small with clear messages.

645
00:55:15,890 --> 00:55:17,810
 Never commit secrets.

646
00:55:17,810 --> 00:55:19,700
 New work equals new branch.

647
00:55:20,330 --> 00:55:22,050
 Main always works.

648
00:55:22,050 --> 00:55:27,280
 Feature branch, then pull request, review and merge.

649
00:55:27,280 --> 00:55:31,970
 A merge conflict is a question, not an error.

650
00:55:31,970 --> 00:55:35,420
 And these are the points to actually remember.

